1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:02,185
Nineteen aircraft.

2
00:00:02,185 --> 00:00:08,709
Every type Japan's military flies in 2026 — counted, one by one.

3
00:00:09,809 --> 00:00:10,872
Number one.

4
00:00:10,872 --> 00:00:16,570
Two hundred Eagles, licence-built by Mitsubishi since 1981,

5
00:00:16,570 --> 00:00:20,723
still the backbone of Japanese air defence.

6
00:00:21,443 --> 00:00:25,468
Ninety F-2s: a bigger wing, heavy anti-ship missiles,

7
00:00:25,468 --> 00:00:28,734
built to break up a landing fleet offshore.

8
00:00:29,454 --> 00:00:36,280
One hundred and forty-seven F-35s planned, with the B models going to sea on Izumo and Kaga.

9
00:00:37,000 --> 00:00:39,239
Only thirty-four Cobras left.

10
00:00:39,239 --> 00:00:43,177
The anti-tank helicopter Japan is quietly retiring.

11
00:00:43,897 --> 00:00:49,179
Twelve Apaches, after costs soared and licence production was halted early.

12
00:00:49,899 --> 00:00:56,726
The Chinook has lifted for Japan since 1986 — artillery, troops, and water over Fukushima.

13
00:00:57,446 --> 00:01:03,112
The Seahawk hunts submarines from destroyers, lowering its dipping sonar into the water.

14
00:01:03,832 --> 00:01:08,510
By August 2025 the whole Osprey fleet had moved to Camp Saga,

15
00:01:08,510 --> 00:01:11,041
closer to the brigade it carries.

16
00:01:11,761 --> 00:01:15,275
Forty P-1s: four engines, fly-by-light controls,

17
00:01:15,275 --> 00:01:19,227
and the first patrol aircraft built entirely in Japan.

18
00:01:19,947 --> 00:01:23,960
The Orion hunted Soviet submarines through the Cold War.

19
00:01:23,960 --> 00:01:25,322
Twenty-four remain.

20
00:01:26,042 --> 00:01:28,845
The C-2 is the homegrown jet airlifter,

21
00:01:28,845 --> 00:01:32,869
built to carry heavy vehicles between scattered islands.

22
00:01:33,589 --> 00:01:40,276
The Hercules has served since 1984, and some now refuel rescue helicopters in flight.

23
00:01:40,996 --> 00:01:45,896
Japan's first tanker, which quietly gave the force a reach it had never had.

24
00:01:46,616 --> 00:01:51,387
And the newer Pegasus, whose camera-guided boom has had teething problems.

25
00:01:52,107 --> 00:02:00,339
Four E-767s lift a radar dome over the horizon and turn the fleet into one network.

26
00:02:01,059 --> 00:02:06,469
Eighteen E-2Ds on order, doing the same job from smaller decks.

27
00:02:07,189 --> 00:02:13,749
The T-4 has trained Japanese pilots for four decades, at a fraction of a fighter's cost.

28
00:02:14,469 --> 00:02:20,262
The US-2 lands on open ocean to pull people out — and flies supplies to Antarctica.

29
00:02:21,032 --> 00:02:22,096
Nineteen types.

30
00:02:22,096 --> 00:02:22,805
One fleet.

31
00:02:22,805 --> 00:02:24,082
One island nation.

